// Section: Navigation & customer comms — generic navigation + chat.
{
  "$schema": "../../../docs/rules.fragment.schema.json",
  "screens": [
    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.nav_arriving",
      "priority": 69,
      "comment": "#745: the arriving_at_title mask is a documented residual with NO normalize — it is store-ambiguous (renders a STORE on a pickup approach, an address/customer on a dropoff approach), so a customer-name canonical key would corrupt a store token; and this surface feeds no store-lineage join, so the mask hex never needs to equal a persisted customerNameHash. Whole-token mask, un-normalized.",
      "redact": [
        {
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "arriving_at_title"
          }
        }
      ],
      "require": {
        "any": [
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasIdSuffix": "arriving_at_subtitle"
            }
          },
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasIdSuffix": "arriving_at_title"
            }
          },
          {
            "exists": {
              "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_arrived_header_v2"
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      "reject": [
        {
          "exists": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "bottom_sheet_task_title"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.navigation_generic",
      "priority": 95,
      "comment": "#886: this is the full-screen nav catch-all — it matches on the maneuver-view ids ALONE, with no task sheet to say whose address is on screen, so it is store-AMBIGUOUS in exactly the nav_arriving sense (#745): the same frame shape carries a merchant approach on a pickup leg and the CUSTOMER's approach on a dropoff leg, where the final maneuver renders the full destination address ('<house#> <Street>, <City>, <ST> <zip>, USA will be on the right'). Fail toward privacy: mask the whole maneuver cluster + the current-road node, un-normalized (an address token is not the customer-NAME key). Over-mask cost is a merchant route line in a debug envelope; under-mask cost is a customer's street address persisted raw. Recognition on replay is unchanged — every anchor here is an ID (maneuverView / mainManeuverLayout) or comes from nodes this block doesn't touch (the 'min'/'exit'/'mi'/'ft' chrome lives in stepDistance / remaining-time / Exit-button nodes).",
      "redact": [
        {
          "find": {
            "any": [
              {
                "hasIdSuffix": "primaryManeuverText"
              },
              {
                "hasIdSuffix": "subManeuverText"
              },
              {
                "hasIdSuffix": "secondaryManeuverText"
              },
              {
                "hasIdSuffix": "roadNameView"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#993 belt: the SAME store-ambiguity argument this rule already makes for the maneuver cluster applies to DoorDash's own `arriving_at_title` arrival banner, which restates the destination — a merchant address on a pickup leg, the CUSTOMER's full street address on a dropoff leg. `nav_arriving` (69) masks it and rejects bottom-sheet frames; this catch-all (95) can win a banner-bearing frame `nav_arriving` rejected and had no entry for it. Fail toward privacy, exactly as the cluster above: whole token, un-normalized (an address is not the customer-NAME key). Over-mask cost is a merchant line in a debug envelope; under-mask cost is a customer's street address persisted raw. Recognition unchanged — every anchor here is an id or lives in nodes this block does not touch.",
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "arriving_at_title"
          }
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1039 belt (the #993 combined-frame precedent): the FUSED subpremise line — `Apt/Suite <n>` or `Apt/Suite: <n>` in ONE id-less node, the render the committed `dropoff_pre_arrival` fixtures carry. Declared on EVERY screen rule in `dropoff.json5` plus THIS nav catch-all — which is in the same class and simply lives in another source file, exactly as #886's maneuver cluster and #993's arrival banner already are — rather than only on the surfaces that fielded it, because a redact protects only the frames its OWN rule wins and the rules most likely to claim an address-block frame OUT-RANK the fielded pair (`delivery_summary_collapsed` 31, `dropoff_navigation` 61, `dropoff_photo` 62, `dropoff_geofence_warning` 84). Entry is `dropoff_photo`'s #986 entry verbatim — see it for the full reasoning: the token after the label strip is a bounded ~10^4 unit number, so a 4-hex distinctness suffix is brute-recoverable (`plainMask`), while the label itself is app vocabulary and is kept for replay context. ACCEPTED COLLATERAL: the split row's BARE `Apt/Suite` LABEL node is itself an exact prefix match here, and no keepPrefix can be kept from it, so it masks whole to plain `[redacted]` — two words of app chrome lost on a debug envelope, the same fail-toward-privacy trade `DropoffBannerRedactParityTest` already documents for the `Arriving at` label. It cannot break the label-sibling entry below: redact predicates are evaluated against the ORIGINAL tree, the mask lands on a copy. Inert on any frame without the line; the #1039 parity test in `CaptureRedactionCorpusTest` pins that every rule declares it, so a new dropoff surface cannot forget.",
          "find": {
            "hasTextStartsWith": "Apt/Suite"
          },
          "keepPrefix": [
            "Apt/Suite: ",
            "Apt/Suite "
          ],
          "plainMask": true
        },
        {
          "comment": "#1039 belt: the LABEL-anchored half of the subpremise mask — an `Apt/Suite` label node followed by its value, whatever spelling that value carries (`Unit 4202` shipped raw 2026-08-14). Both label spellings are enumerated because `hasPrecedingSiblingText` is an exact, un-trimmed equality; whole-node `plainMask` because a unit number is a bounded ~10^4 alphabet. Ordered ahead of every generic shape entry in this block: `maskNode` is first-match-wins, and a digit-leading value (`101 B`) would otherwise be claimed and HASHED by the id-less street shape. See `dropoff_handoff` (dropoff.json5) for the full reasoning. Inert without the label; pinned by the #1039 parity test.",
          "find": {
            "any": [
              {
                "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Apt/Suite"
              },
              {
                "hasPrecedingSiblingText": "Apt/Suite:"
              }
            ]
          },
          "plainMask": true
        }
      ],
      "branches": [
        {
          "state": {
            "flow": "idle",
            "modeHint": "online"
          },
          "reject": [
            {
              "allTextContains": "accept"
            },
            {
              "allTextContains": "decline"
            }
          ],
          "require": {
            "all": [
              {
                "allTextContainsAll": [
                  "min",
                  "exit"
                ]
              },
              {
                "allTextContainsAny": [
                  "mi",
                  "ft"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "state": {
            "modeHint": "online"
          },
          "reject": [
            {
              "allTextContains": "accept"
            },
            {
              "allTextContains": "decline"
            }
          ],
          "require": {
            "exists": {
              "any": [
                {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "maneuverView"
                },
                {
                  "hasIdSuffix": "mainManeuverLayout"
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.chat",
      "priority": 92,
      "reject": [
        {
          "allTextContains": "folder"
        }
      ],
      "require": {
        "allTextContainsAll": [
          "dasher",
          "messages"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "doordash.screen.chat_conversation",
      "priority": 89,
      "comment": "#745: the chat nav-bar title IS the customer name → normalize so its mask hex matches the canonical-key customerNameHash the delivery surfaces persist (one customer masks identically across chat + dropoff). The message bodies are free customer text, not a name to join on — masked whole, un-normalized (canonicalizing a sentence would be meaningless).",
      "redact": [
        {
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "textView_navBar_title"
          },
          "normalize": "customerName"
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "message_self_message"
          }
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "message_other_message"
          }
        },
        {
          "find": {
            "hasIdSuffix": "admin_message_text"
          }
        }
      ],
      "require": {
        "exists": {
          "any": [
            {
              "hasIdSuffix": "ddchat_holder_base"
            },
            {
              "hasIdSuffix": "inputChannelView"
            },
            {
              "hasIdSuffix": "message_recycler_view"
            },
            {
              "hasIdSuffix": "message_self_message"
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  "clicks": [
    {
      "id": "doordash.click.open_chat",
      "priority": 45,
      "intent": "open_chat",
      "require": {
        "hasIdSuffix": "chat_button_internal"
      }
    }
  ]
}
